    "description": "A cooperative harbor game contains patrolling guards, animated statues, homing projectiles, and crowd agents. Each actor type has its own recurring behavior: GuardPatrol chooses a route, StatueCharge accumulates energy, ProjectileMotion steers toward a target, and CrowdAgent selects a local goal. The central FrameDriver currently contains type checks and inline blocks for all four behaviors, making it difficult to add an actor without editing the driver. Each live participant must advance exactly once per simulation tick, in a deterministic registry order, and newly spawned actors must not be advanced twice in the same tick. A behavior must also be able to request removal without invalidating the current traversal. Input polling and presentation must continue while actors advance, and no actor may run an unbounded private loop that prevents other actors from progressing. The design must establish a clear home for actor-specific recurring behavior and a uniform way for the driver to invoke it across heterogeneous object types.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose where recurring actor-specific behavior resides and define the uniform contract by which the frame driver advances every live participant exactly once.",
    "notes": "Update-method fits because each actor type owns its recurring per-tick step behind one driver dispatch contract with exactly-once and safe-removal rules; game-loop is the tick-framing sibling and double-buffer supports safe spawn and removal."
  },
